1
00:00:00,848 --> 00:00:08,824
Welcome to the Europe capitals, episode four of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,486 --> 00:00:21,386
Eight capitals lie along the Alpine arc: Liechtenstein and Switzerland, then
Slovenia and Croatia, and the Italian peninsula with its two tiny states.

3
00:00:22,047 --> 00:00:24,044
Let's bring Vaduz into view.

4
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:34,170
Vaduz lies in the Rhine valley in the west of Liechtenstein, between the Alps
and the river.

5
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:47,033
The prince of Liechtenstein lives in the castle above the town, and it is the
capital of one of only two countries surrounded entirely by landlocked
countries.

6
00:00:47,686 --> 00:00:54,722
Remember Vaduz as the town under a prince's castle, in one of the only two
countries with no coast at all.

7
00:00:55,409 --> 00:00:57,452
Let's bring Bern into view.

8
00:01:02,084 --> 00:01:08,562
Bern lies in the Swiss plateau on the Aare river, in the west of Switzerland.

9
00:01:09,213 --> 00:01:16,968
By legend its founder named it for the first animal he killed in the woods, a
bear, and bears are still kept in the town.

10
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:29,346
Remember Bern as the bear city that is officially not the capital but the
"federal city", where Einstein wrote his papers of 1905.

11
00:01:30,023 --> 00:01:32,403
Let's bring Ljubljana into view.

12
00:01:36,436 --> 00:01:43,472
Ljubljana stands on the Ljubljanica river in central Slovenia, where the Alps
rise to the north.

13
00:01:44,135 --> 00:01:52,146
Where the name comes from is disputed; a legend puts Jason and the Argonauts
here, killing the dragon now on its arms.

14
00:01:52,801 --> 00:02:02,774
Remember Ljubljana as the city of the dragon, reshaped between the wars by
Jože Plečnik and named European Green Capital in 2016.

15
00:02:03,455 --> 00:02:06,009
Let's bring Zagreb into view.

16
00:02:09,561 --> 00:02:16,933
Zagreb lies in the north-west of Croatia, on the Sava river, at the foot of
the Medvednica hills.

17
00:02:17,597 --> 00:02:28,476
Where the name comes from is uncertain; a folk story reads it as "behind the
hill". It grew from two hill towns, Gradec and Kaptol, joined in 1850.

18
00:02:29,129 --> 00:02:39,578
Remember Zagreb as the city behind the hill, grown from two towns joined in
1850, and home of the Museum of Broken Relationships.

19
00:02:40,270 --> 00:02:42,522
Let's bring San Marino into view.

20
00:02:46,471 --> 00:02:54,099
San Marino stands on a steep hill in central Italy, in the Emilia-Romagna
region of the peninsula.

21
00:02:54,758 --> 00:03:05,381
Named for Saint Marinus, a stonemason who by tradition founded it in 301, and
it claims to be the oldest surviving republic in the world.

22
00:03:06,041 --> 00:03:16,107
Remember San Marino as the republic founded by a stonemason in 301, with its
three towers along the ridge of Monte Titano.

23
00:03:16,792 --> 00:03:18,580
Let's bring Rome into view.

24
00:03:23,248 --> 00:03:30,841
Rome, called Roma in Italian, stands on the Tiber river in central Italy, in
the middle of the Italian peninsula.

25
00:03:31,490 --> 00:03:39,594
Legend says Romulus; more likely from Etruscan Ruma, a family name, or from an
old word for the river.

26
00:03:40,253 --> 00:03:51,642
Remember Rome as the largest city in the world for five centuries, the first
to reach a million people, and the home of the Pantheon's unreinforced
concrete dome.

27
00:03:52,339 --> 00:03:54,255
Let's bring Monaco into view.

28
00:03:59,305 --> 00:04:05,528
Monaco-Ville stands on a steep headland on the Mediterranean coast, at the
border of France.

29
00:04:06,187 --> 00:04:15,997
From Greek Monoikos, "dwelling alone", a title of Heracles, who had a temple
here. The Grimaldi family has held it since 1297.

30
00:04:16,653 --> 00:04:27,822
Remember Monaco as the dwelling alone of Heracles, taken by the Grimaldis in
1297 and raced through its streets since 1929.

31
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:31,933
Let's bring Andorra la Vella into view.

32
00:04:34,974 --> 00:04:43,074
Andorra la Vella stands in a high valley in the Pyrenees, in the centre of
Andorra, where the Valira river runs.

33
00:04:43,726 --> 00:04:53,107
"Andorra the Old", the oldest settlement of the valleys. At about 1,023 metres
it is the highest capital city in Europe.

34
00:04:53,765 --> 00:05:02,809
Remember Andorra la Vella as the highest capital in Europe, and the Casa de la
Vall, a 16th-century house that was its parliament for three centuries.

35
00:05:08,388 --> 00:05:23,353
Vaduz and Bern sit in the Alps; Ljubljana and Zagreb are just south of them.
San Marino, Rome and Monaco run down the Italian peninsula, with Andorra la
Vella across the mountains to the west.

36
00:05:24,005 --> 00:05:46,001
Remember the prince's castle and the landlocked Liechtenstein, the bear city
of Bern and Einstein's year of 1905, the dragon of Ljubljana, the hill behind
Zagreb, the stonemason of San Marino, the twins of Rome, the dwelling alone of
Heracles, and the highest capital in Europe.
